```rust
/// Marker appended to one `/mcp list` row whose server failed to connect.
///
/// Pure: it takes the never-drained `mcp_failed` snapshot and the **one** row's
/// identity string, and answers whether *that* row is the failed one.
///
/// `None` when `mcp_failed` is empty — every user whose servers all connect
/// (and every user who configured none) renders byte-identically to before.
///
/// **It marks only what it can prove.** `mcp_connected` in the aggregate line is
/// a *count*, not a set, so the rows that did connect are not identified
/// anywhere; a per-row "connected" claim would be the confident-wrong-diagnosis
/// defect this whole surface exists to avoid. Only the recorded failures get a
/// marker, and the aggregate tail keeps carrying the count exactly as before.
///
/// **It names the server and never a tool.** A failed connect means yoyo never
/// learned which tools that server exposes, so no tool name is invented.
///
/// **MCP only.** `openapi_failed` is a different surface with its own rows and
/// deliberately does not appear here — the same rule `connections_lost_note`
/// follows when it carries `kind` rather than folding two kinds together.
///
/// Glyph-free under `is_plain_output()` (no ✗), matching
/// `project_mcp_refusal_message` / `collision_guard_skipped_message` /
/// `connections_lost_note`.
pub(crate) fn mcp_failure_marker(mcp_failed: &[String], row_id: &str, plain: bool) -> Option<String> {
    if !mcp_failed.iter().any(|id| id == row_id) {
        return None;
    }
    let id = crate::agent_builder::capped_failure_id(row_id);
    Some(if plain {
        format!("  failed to connect: {id}")
    } else {
        format!("  \u{2717} failed to connect: {id}")
    })
}
```

